Cache Stampede, Hot Keys & Cache Failure Internals 
Module 2 — Caching
Lesson 32.5 — Cache Stampede, Hot Keys & Cache Failure Internals
We are now at the failure-engineering part of caching.
So far we learned:
Cache
 ├── stores copies
 ├── expires entries
 ├── evicts entries
 └── needs invalidation
Now ask the harder production question:
What happens when thousands or millions of requests hit the cache at exactly the wrong time?
There are three major problems:
1. Cache Stampede
2. Hot Key
3. Cache Failure
They are related, but they are not the same problem.
________________________________________
1. Cache Stampede
Imagine this system:
                    ┌─────────┐
Requests ──────────►│  Redis  │
                    └────┬────┘
                         │
                       MISS
                         │
                         ▼
                    ┌─────────┐
                    │   DB    │
                    └─────────┘
Normally:
100,000 requests/sec
        │
        ▼
      Redis
        │
        ├── 99,000 HIT
        │
        └── 1,000 MISS
                 │
                 ▼
                 DB
Database is happy.
Now imagine a very popular key:
product:123
TTL:
60 seconds
At exactly 12:00:00:
product:123 expires
Suddenly:
100,000 requests
        │
        ▼
      Redis
        │
      MISS
        │
        ▼
   100,000 DB requests
That is the fundamental cache-stampede problem.
________________________________________
2. Why this is more dangerous than it looks
The database suddenly receives:
100,000 requests/sec
It may normally handle:
10,000 requests/sec
So:
DB overload
   ↓
latency increases
   ↓
timeouts
   ↓
application threads wait
   ↓
connection pool fills
   ↓
requests queue
   ↓
more timeouts
And then something particularly nasty can happen.
________________________________________
3. Retry amplification
Suppose the application has:
retry = 2
A database request times out.
The application retries:
Request
   ↓
DB
   ↓
timeout
   ↓
retry
   ↓
DB
   ↓
timeout
   ↓
retry
   ↓
DB
One original request can become:
1 → 3 database requests
So:
100,000 requests
       ×
3 attempts
       =
300,000 DB operations
The system is now making the problem worse.
This is why we'll later study:
Retry
Timeout
Circuit Breaker
Bulkhead
Rate Limiting
as a connected system rather than isolated patterns.
________________________________________
4. The first solution: Single Flight
The basic idea is:
For a particular key, allow only one request to load the missing value.
Suppose:
100 requests
    │
    ▼
Redis MISS
Instead of:
100 → DB
we want:
1 → DB
99 → wait
Flow:
                 Redis MISS
                     │
              ┌──────┴──────┐
              │             │
          First request   Others
              │             │
              ▼             │
             DB              │
              │             │
              ▼             │
           Redis             │
              │             │
              └──────┬──────┘
                     ▼
                  result
This is request coalescing / single-flight.
________________________________________
5. Java implementation idea
Suppose:
ConcurrentHashMap<String, CompletableFuture<Product>>
stores currently-loading keys.
Conceptually:
public Product get(String key) {

    Product cached = redis.get(key);

    if (cached != null) {
        return cached;
    }

    CompletableFuture<Product> future =
        inFlight.computeIfAbsent(
            key,
            k -> loadFromDatabase(k)
        );

    try {
        return future.join();
    } finally {
        inFlight.remove(key, future);
    }
}
The important idea is not the exact code.
It is:
same key
   ↓
same in-flight operation
________________________________________
6. Why computeIfAbsent is interesting
Suppose 1,000 Java threads execute:
inFlight.computeIfAbsent("product:123", ...)
concurrently.
The data structure coordinates access so that the application can establish one shared computation for the key.
Conceptually:
Thread 1 ─┐
Thread 2 ─┤
Thread 3 ─┤
Thread 4 ─┤
...       ├──► product:123 future
Thread N ─┘
Now they can all wait on the same future.
This is an excellent example of using a concurrency primitive to solve a distributed-performance problem.
________________________________________
7. But single-flight has a boundary
Suppose your application has:
Instance A
Instance B
Instance C
Instance D
Each JVM has its own:
inFlight
Then:
Requests
   │
   ├── A → one DB request
   ├── B → one DB request
   ├── C → one DB request
   └── D → one DB request
So instead of:
1 DB request
you may still get:
4 DB requests
This is because the coordination is local to each JVM.
________________________________________
8. Distributed single-flight
Now imagine:
10 application instances
You want one global loader.
You could use a distributed coordination mechanism:
Application
     │
     ▼
Distributed Lock
     │
     ▼
Redis
     │
     ▼
Database
Only one instance gets permission to load:
Instance A → LOCK SUCCESS → DB
Instance B → LOCK FAIL → wait
Instance C → LOCK FAIL → wait
This connects directly to our future topic:
Distributed Lock
We will study its internals separately.
________________________________________
9. But distributed locks introduce complexity
Suppose A gets the lock:
A
 ↓
LOCK
 ↓
DB
Then A crashes.
Who releases the lock?
If the lock never expires:
everyone waits forever
So distributed locks generally need:
lock TTL
ownership
safe release
failure handling
Now we have introduced another distributed-systems problem.
This is why:
Don't automatically reach for a distributed lock just because you have a cache miss.
Sometimes local single-flight + TTL + stale serving is simpler and safer.
________________________________________
10. Stale-While-Revalidate
Another approach is even more interesting.
Instead of:
expired → MISS → DB → wait
we allow:
stale → immediately return → refresh asynchronously
Example:
Product price = ₹100

soft TTL = 55 sec
hard TTL = 60 sec
At 56 seconds:
Request
   ↓
Cache
   ↓
stale-but-servable
   ↓
return ₹100
Meanwhile:
background refresh
       ↓
      DB
       ↓
     ₹105
       ↓
    Redis
Next request:
₹105
This avoids a huge synchronous load.
________________________________________
11. Why stale data can be valuable
Consider:
Netflix movie metadata
Being 5 seconds stale:
probably acceptable
But waiting 5 seconds because the cache is rebuilding:
bad user experience
So you can trade:
freshness
for:
availability + latency
This is a fundamental distributed-system trade-off.
________________________________________
12. Now let's move to Hot Keys
A hot key is different from a stampede.
Suppose:
product:123
receives:
200,000 requests/sec
and the key is perfectly cached.
There is no cache miss.
Still:
200,000 requests
        │
        ▼
 product:123
One key receives a disproportionate amount of traffic.
That's a hot key.
________________________________________
13. Hot key vs cache stampede
This distinction is important.
Stampede
Cache MISS
   ↓
many requests
   ↓
DB
Hot key
Cache HIT
   ↓
huge traffic concentrated on one key
They can happen simultaneously:
HOT KEY
   ↓
expires
   ↓
STAMPede
That's particularly dangerous.
________________________________________
14. Why a hot key can overload Redis
Suppose Redis cluster:
Node A
Node B
Node C
Node D
Keys are distributed:
A → Node A
B → Node B
C → Node C
D → Node D
But:
product:123
maps to:
Node B
Now:
200,000 req/sec
       ↓
    Node B
while:
Node A → 10k
Node C → 12k
Node D → 8k
The cluster overall may have capacity.
But:
Node B = bottleneck
This is a classic distributed-systems problem:
Average load can look healthy while one partition is overloaded.
________________________________________
15. Key distribution doesn't solve hot keys
Consistent hashing distributes keys.
It does not automatically distribute requests for the same key.
This is a very important distinction.
Suppose:
1 million keys
distributed evenly.
But:
one key = 70% of traffic
You can still have a severe hotspot.
We'll study consistent hashing deeply later.
________________________________________
16. Solution: Replicate the hot value
Suppose:
product:123
is extremely popular.
Instead of one cache location:
product:123 → Node B
you can create replicas:
product:123:replica1 → Node A
product:123:replica2 → Node B
product:123:replica3 → Node C
product:123:replica4 → Node D
Then requests can be distributed:
             product:123
                  │
        ┌─────────┼─────────┐
        ▼         ▼         ▼
       A          B          C
Now the load is spread.
________________________________________
17. But replication creates a consistency problem
Suppose price changes:
₹100 → ₹105
Now:
Replica A = 105
Replica B = 100
Replica C = 100
Replica D = 105
You've exchanged:
hotspot problem
for:
replica consistency problem
Again:
Distributed systems are mostly about trade-offs.
There is rarely a free optimization.
________________________________________
18. Another solution: Local cache
Architecture:
Application
    │
    ▼
Local JVM Cache
    │
    ▼
Redis
    │
    ▼
Database
For an extremely hot key:
Request
  ↓
JVM memory
  ↓
HIT
The request doesn't even reach Redis.
For example:
100,000 req/sec
might become:
95,000 → local cache
5,000  → Redis
This dramatically reduces network traffic.
________________________________________
19. But local cache creates another consistency layer
Now we have:
Database
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
JVM Cache
Three copies.
Potentially:
DB       = 105
Redis    = 105
JVM A    = 100
JVM B    = 105
JVM C    = 100
Now invalidation must propagate through more layers.
So local caching improves:
latency
throughput
Redis load
but increases:
consistency complexity
________________________________________
20. Now the most dangerous problem: Cache Failure
Suppose everything is working:
Application
     ↓
   Redis
     ↓
    DB
Redis suddenly becomes unavailable.
What does the application do?
There are two broad choices.
________________________________________
21. Fail-open
Fail-open means:
If cache fails, continue to the underlying system.
Request
   ↓
Redis
   X
   ↓
DB
This preserves availability of the application.
But there is a huge danger.
Suppose:
100,000 req/sec
normally Redis handles almost all of them.
Redis dies.
Now:
100,000 req/sec
       ↓
       DB
The database may immediately collapse.
So:
Fail-open can turn a cache failure into a database failure.
________________________________________
22. Fail-closed
Fail-closed means:
If the cache is unavailable, don't continue normally.
For some operations:
Redis failure
    ↓
return error
This protects the database.
But:
availability ↓
So:
Fail-open
→ better availability
→ potentially catastrophic DB load

Fail-closed
→ protects backend
→ more application errors
The correct choice depends on the business operation.
________________________________________
23. Why fallback is dangerous
Imagine:
Redis capacity = 200,000 req/sec
DB capacity    = 20,000 req/sec
Normally:
200k requests
     ↓
Redis
     ↓
99% hit
     ↓
DB ≈ 2k
Redis fails:
200k
 ↓
DB
DB can only process:
20k
The remaining:
180k
start waiting.
Then connection pools fill.
________________________________________
24. Connection pool exhaustion
Suppose Spring Boot has:
DB connection pool = 100
Requests arrive:
200,000/sec
but DB is slow.
Threads start waiting for connections:
Thread 1 → connection
Thread 2 → connection
...
Thread 100 → connection
Thread 101 → WAIT
Thread 102 → WAIT
...
Eventually:
request timeout
The application becomes unhealthy even though:
Java CPU may not be 100%
This is why resource exhaustion matters more than CPU alone.
________________________________________
25. The fallback death spiral
This sequence is worth memorizing as a system behavior, not a definition:
Redis failure
     ↓
Cache misses
     ↓
DB traffic explodes
     ↓
DB latency increases
     ↓
Connections stay occupied longer
     ↓
Connection pool exhausts
     ↓
Requests timeout
     ↓
Retry starts
     ↓
DB traffic increases further
     ↓
DB becomes even slower
     ↓
More timeouts
This is a positive feedback loop.
The system is feeding its own failure.
________________________________________
26. Protection: Circuit Breaker
Suppose DB is clearly failing.
Instead of continuing:
request
 ↓
DB
 ↓
timeout
a circuit breaker can eventually say:
DB unhealthy
     ↓
OPEN
     ↓
reject/fail fast
Now requests don't keep consuming:
threads
connections
network
This is why circuit breakers are useful downstream of caches.
We'll study the internal state machine later.
________________________________________
27. Protection: Rate Limiting
Suppose after Redis failure:
200,000 req/sec
hits your database.
Instead:
allow only 10,000/sec
The rest are rejected or delayed.
This sacrifices some requests to protect the entire system.
This is the fundamental idea behind:
Load shedding.
________________________________________
28. Protection: Bulkhead
Suppose your application serves:
product requests
payment requests
profile requests
If product cache failure consumes every thread:
product → 100% threads
payment → 0
profile → 0
That's bad.
Bulkhead isolation can give separate resource pools:
Product → 50 threads
Payment → 30 threads
Profile → 20 threads
Now product failure doesn't necessarily consume everything.
________________________________________
29. A robust failure architecture
A more resilient system could look like:
                 Request
                    │
                    ▼
              Local Cache
                    │
                  MISS
                    │
                    ▼
                 Redis
                    │
              ┌─────┴─────┐
             HIT          FAIL
              │             │
              ▼             ▼
           return       protection
                            │
                  ┌─────────┼─────────┐
                  ▼         ▼         ▼
              RateLimit  Circuit   Bulkhead
                            │
                            ▼
                           DB
This is much closer to production engineering.
________________________________________
30. What if Redis is slow rather than completely down?
This is even more dangerous.
Suppose Redis normally:
P99 = 2 ms
Suddenly:
P99 = 2 seconds
Requests wait for Redis.
Application threads become occupied.
Even though Redis eventually responds:
the application can still become overloaded.
So:
Dependency latency can be as dangerous as dependency failure.
________________________________________
31. Cache timeout
Never allow an external cache call to wait indefinitely.
Conceptually:
redis.get(key)
should have a bounded timeout.
For example:
Redis timeout = 50 ms
Then:
Redis doesn't respond
        ↓
stop waiting
        ↓
fallback / fail
The exact timeout must be based on your latency budget.
Don't blindly use arbitrary numbers.
________________________________________
32. Why timeout + retry can be disastrous
Suppose:
Redis timeout = 100ms
retry = 3
Worst-case waiting can approach:
100ms
+
100ms
+
100ms
before accounting for backoff and other overhead.
Now multiply by:
10,000 concurrent requests
You've tied up a large number of resources waiting for a failing dependency.
Retry isn't automatically resilience.
Retry can amplify failure.
________________________________________
33. Connection pool to Redis
Redis clients also have connection pools.
Suppose:
Redis pool = 50 connections
and Redis becomes slow.
Those 50 connections are occupied.
Additional application threads wait:
Thread
  ↓
Redis connection pool
  ↓
WAIT
So you can get:
Redis slowdown
    ↓
Redis pool exhaustion
    ↓
application threads waiting
    ↓
latency explosion
Again, resource exhaustion is the real issue.
________________________________________
34. Cache failure is not just "Redis is down"
There are multiple failure modes:
Redis completely unavailable
Redis network partition
Redis high latency
Redis connection pool exhausted
Redis memory pressure
Redis evictions
Redis CPU saturation
single hot key
single hot shard
serialization overhead
network congestion
Each has different symptoms.
A senior engineer learns to distinguish them.
________________________________________
35. Cache metrics you should look at
Suppose users report:
"The application became slow."
Don't immediately blame the database.
Look at:
Cache hit ratio
Cache latency P50
Cache latency P95
Cache latency P99
Cache errors
Connection pool usage
Eviction rate
Memory usage
Hot-key distribution
Redis CPU
Redis network throughput
DB QPS
DB connection pool usage
Then correlate the timeline.
________________________________________
36. Example production incident
Imagine:
10:00
Redis P99 = 2 ms
DB QPS = 5,000
At 10:01:
Redis P99 = 500 ms
At 10:02:
DB QPS = 50,000
At 10:03:
DB P99 = 2 sec
At 10:04:
DB connections = 100/100
At 10:05:
application timeouts
A superficial diagnosis:
"Database is slow."
Better diagnosis:
Redis degradation
      ↓
cache requests slow/fail
      ↓
fallback traffic ↑
      ↓
DB overloaded
The database was a victim, not necessarily the root cause.
________________________________________
37. Hot key + failure is even worse
Consider:
product:123
receives:
200k req/sec
It lives on:
Redis Node B
Node B fails.
Now all requests for the hot key need another path.
If the application falls back to DB:
200k req/sec
    ↓
DB
One hot key can therefore become a database attack against your own system.
________________________________________
38. Local cache as protection
A local cache can act as a shock absorber.
Architecture:
Request
   ↓
L1: JVM cache
   ↓ miss
L2: Redis
   ↓ miss
L3: Database
This is sometimes called multi-level caching.
L1 = fastest
L2 = shared
L3 = source of truth
Example:
L1 hit → ~microseconds
L2 hit → network round trip
DB hit → much slower
Exact latency depends on infrastructure, but the ordering is what matters.
________________________________________
39. But L1 creates stale copies
Suppose:
DB = 200
Redis = 200
JVM A = 100
If invalidation isn't propagated:
JVM A
  ↓
returns 100
Therefore multi-level caches often need:
invalidation events
or carefully bounded TTLs.
This is where:
Caching
+
Messaging
+
Redis
begin to converge.
________________________________________
40. The three-layer failure model
Think about:
L1
 ↓
L2
 ↓
L3
At every level, ask:
What if it misses?
What if it is slow?
What if it is unavailable?
What if it contains stale data?
What if traffic concentrates here?
This mindset is much more valuable than memorizing cache patterns.
________________________________________
41. A real design decision
Suppose you're designing a product catalog.
Requirements:
100k requests/sec
Product data changes occasionally
5-second stale data is acceptable
Database can handle 10k/sec
A reasonable design might be:
             Requests
                 │
                 ▼
            Local Cache
                 │
                 ▼
              Redis
                 │
                 ▼
                DB
Use:
TTL
+
stale-while-revalidate
+
single-flight
+
TTL jitter
If Redis fails:
serve local cached data if possible
If local cache misses:
bounded fallback to DB
with:
timeouts
circuit breaker
rate limiting
That's system design.
________________________________________
42. But don't apply every pattern blindly
This is important for your learning path.
For every cache problem, don't automatically add:
Redis
Kafka
Distributed Lock
Circuit Breaker
Local Cache
Rate Limiter
Every additional mechanism introduces:
complexity
failure modes
operational cost
consistency problems
Start with the simplest architecture that satisfies the requirement.
________________________________________
43. Decision tree
When a cache miss happens:
                 Cache MISS
                     │
                     ▼
             Is key popular?
                /       \
              NO         YES
              │           │
              ▼           ▼
          DB load     Stampede risk?
                         │
                     ┌───┴───┐
                    NO      YES
                    │         │
                    ▼         ▼
                  load     single-flight
                              │
                              ▼
                       distributed?
                         /       \
                       NO         YES
                       │           │
                       ▼           ▼
                   local SF    distributed
                                coordination
Then separately:
Cache HIT
   │
   ▼
Is key extremely hot?
   │
  YES
   │
   ▼
L1 cache / replication / sharding strategy
And:
Cache unavailable
       │
       ▼
Can stale data be served?
     /       \
   YES       NO
    │         │
    ▼         ▼
serve stale  bounded fallback
              │
              ▼
        circuit/rate limit
This is the reasoning framework I want you to develop.
________________________________________
44. The deepest takeaway
Caching isn't fundamentally about:
"put data in Redis"
It is about managing:
              ┌──────────────┐
              │   Database   │
              │ source truth │
              └──────┬───────┘
                     │
               consistency
                     │
              ┌──────▼───────┐
              │    Cache     │
              └──────┬───────┘
                     │
             performance
                     │
              ┌──────▼───────┐
              │  Application │
              └──────────────┘
while controlling:
staleness
load
latency
memory
failure
concurrency
hotspots
That's why caching is a distributed-systems problem, not merely a performance trick.

